---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../config";

const aboutUrl = new URL("/about/", SITE.url).href;

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  url: aboutUrl,
  mainEntity: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.url,
    description: SITE.author.bio,
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Crochet Explained is an independent site of crochet tutorials and pattern guides, published by the Crochet Explained Editorial Team."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 sm:px-8 py-10 sm:py-14">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
      About {SITE.name}
    </h1>

    <div class="prose mt-8">
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is an independent website dedicated to making crochet
        patterns, tutorials, and creative projects easier to discover and
        understand.
      </p>
      <p>
        We publish step-by-step crochet guides, practical project ideas, and
        seasonal inspiration for makers of different skill levels.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our content may be developed with assistance from digital tools. We aim
        to provide clear, useful instructions and continually improve our
        guides.
      </p>
      <p>Our editorial identity is {SITE.author.name}.</p>

      <h2>{SITE.author.name}</h2>
      <p>{SITE.author.bio}</p>

      <h2>How articles are developed</h2>
      <p>
        Technique pages use standard US crochet terminology, the terms used by
        the Craft Yarn Council, because that is what most patterns written for
        American crocheters use. Where a UK term names a different stitch, the
        page says so.
      </p>
      <p>
        Conversion charts are checked against published standards, such as
        manufacturer hook sizing and Craft Yarn Council yarn weights, rather
        than copied from other blogs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Digital tools may help draft, edit, or illustrate a guide. Illustrations
        made that way are labeled in the caption. A pattern page does not claim
        the project was handmade or yarn-tested unless that is stated on the
        page itself.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If a stitch count, measurement, or step looks wrong, tell us through the
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> and name the page. Reported errors
        are checked and, when the report is right, the page is corrected. When
        a published fact changes, the article's updated date changes with it.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site will not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Republish another designer's pattern. Patterns are their authors'
          work. When a pattern is featured here, the link goes to the designer's
          own page so they get the visit.
        </li>
        <li>
          Pretend a photograph is something it is not. Where an illustration or
          diagram is computer-generated, it is labelled as such on the page.
        </li>
        <li>
          Recommend a product nobody has any reason to recommend. The
          <a href="/disclosures/">disclosures page</a> explains exactly how the
          gear guides work.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How the site pays for itself</h2>
      <p>
        Two ways: display advertising, and affiliate links in the hooks and yarn
        guides. Both are disclosed on the <a href="/disclosures/">disclosures page</a
        >, and neither one costs you anything. Every tutorial and chart on this
        site is free and always will be.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
